'''Janet Trafton Mills''' (born December 30, 1947) is an American politician and attorney who has served since 2019 as the 75th [[governor of Maine]]. She previously served as the 55th and 57th [[Maine Attorney General|attorney general of Maine]] and as a member of the [[Maine House of Representatives]]. She is a member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]].

Mills was first elected attorney general by the [[Maine Legislature]] in 2009 and served until 2011. When Democrats regained control of the legislature, she was elected again in 2013, serving three more two-year terms. In [[2018 Maine gubernatorial election|2018]], Mills was elected governor, defeating [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Shawn Moody]] and independent [[Terry Hayes (politician)|Terry Hayes]] after winning a crowded primary.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://bangordailynews.com/2018/11/07/politics/shawn-moody-concedes-governors-race-to-janet-mills/|title=Janet Mills wins race to succeed LePage as Maine's next governor|work=Bangor Daily News|access-date=November 7, 2018|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181107191137/https://bangordailynews.com/2018/11/07/politics/shawn-moody-concedes-governors-race-to-janet-mills/|archive-date=November 7, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> She was reelected in [[2022 Maine gubernatorial election|2022]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.themainemonitor.org/as-red-wave-fails-to-materialize-nationally-janet-mills-wins-re-election-as-maines-governor/|title=As 'red wave' fails to materialize nationally, Janet Mills wins re-election as Maine's governor|work=The Maine Monitor|access-date=November 9, 2022|language=en-US}}</ref>

Mills ran for the Democratic nomination in the [[2026 United States Senate election in Maine]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite news |last1=Goldmacher |first1=Shane |last2=Russell |first2=Jenna |date=2025-10-14 |title=Gov. Janet Mills of Maine to Run for Senate, Aiming at Senator Susan Collins |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/14/us/politics/janet-mills-senate-race-maine-susan-collins.html |access-date=2025-10-14 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en}}</ref> She later suspended her campaign<ref name="MillsWithdrew">{{cite news |last1=Lerer |first1=Lisa |last2=Glueck |first2=Katie |last3=Epstein |first3=Reid J. |title=Janet Mills, Governor of Maine, Suspends Senate Campaign |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/30/us/politics/janet-mills-drops-out-senate-race-platner-schumer.html |access-date=April 30, 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=April 30, 2026}}</ref> and was defeated in the Democratic primary by [[Graham Platner]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Smith |first=David |date=2026-06-10 |title=Graham Platner shrugs off scandals to win Maine Democratic Senate primary |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jun/09/graham-platner-maine-senate-primary |access-date=2026-06-11 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Mills was born in [[Farmington, Maine]], on December 30, 1947, the daughter of Katherine Louise (Coffin) and [[S. Peter Mills Jr.|Sumner Peter Mills Jr.]]<ref name=Bates>{{cite web |url=https://scarab.bates.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1266&context=muskie_oh |title=Mills, Janet oral history interview |last=Nicoll |first=Don |date= December 20, 1999 |website= Bates.edu |publisher= Bates College |location= Lewiston, ME |page= 2 |access-date= November 29, 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181129184354/https://scarab.bates.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1266&context=muskie_oh |archive-date= November 29, 2018 |url-status= live }}</ref><ref name=Glass>{{cite web|url= https://www.journaltribune.com/articles/front-page/janet-mills-mission-break-yet-another-glass-ceiling/|title=Janet Mills' mission: Break yet another glass ceiling - Journal Tribune|work= Journal Tribune |date= September 23, 2018|access-date= November 11, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180924115903/https://www.journaltribune.com/articles/front-page/janet-mills-mission-break-yet-another-glass-ceiling/ |archive-date= September 24, 2018|url-status= live}}</ref> Her mother was a schoolteacher and [[Congregationalist]],<ref name= "Hanstein">{{cite web | last= Hanstein | first= Bobbie | title=Kay Mills' life remembered and celebrated – Daily Bulldog | website=Daily Bulldog – Franklin County's First News | date= October 16, 2010 | url=https://dailybulldog.com/features/kay-mills-life-remembered-and-celebrated/ | access-date= November 13, 2022}}</ref> while her father was an attorney who served as U.S. Attorney for Maine in the 1950s.<ref name=Bates/> Mills graduated from Farmington High School in 1965. As a teenager, she spent nearly a year bedridden in a [[Orthopedic cast#Body|full-body cast]] due to severe [[scoliosis]], which was corrected surgically.<ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.newscentermaine.com/video/news/politics/janet-mills-family-looking-forward-to-inauguration/97-e8dd74e3-d370-4861-8af4-1df2fd1a51ea |title= Janet Mills' family looking forward to inauguration| website= WCSH| date= January 2019 |access-date= March 5, 2019|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190306044847/https://www.newscentermaine.com/video/news/politics/janet-mills-family-looking-forward-to-inauguration/97-e8dd74e3-d370-4861-8af4-1df2fd1a51ea|archive-date= March 6, 2019|url-status= live}}</ref>

Mills briefly attended [[Colby College]], before moving to [[San Francisco]],<ref name="Bates"/> where she worked as a nursing assistant in a psychiatric hospital.<ref name="Glass"/> In 1970, she earned a [[Bachelor of Arts|BA]] from the [[University of Massachusetts Boston]].<ref name="Bates" /> During her time at UMass Boston, Mills traveled through Western Europe and became fluent in French.<ref>{{cite news |last=Solloway |first=Steve |date=October 30, 2018 |title=The rebel with a cause: Supporters believe Janet Mills' experience, family legacy and legal mind have prepared her well to be Maine's first female governor |url=https://pinetreewatch.org/janet-mills-the-rebel-with-a-cause/ |work=Pine Tree Watch |location=Hallowell, ME |access-date=November 30, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181130155103/https://pinetreewatch.org/janet-mills-the-rebel-with-a-cause/ |archive-date=November 30, 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref> In 1973, she began attending the [[University of Maine School of Law]],<ref name="Bates" /> and in 1974 was a summer intern in Washington, D.C., for civil rights attorney [[Charles Morgan Jr.]] of the [[American Civil Liberties Union]].<ref name="Bates"/> Mills received a [[Juris Doctor]] degree in 1976, and was admitted to the bar.<ref name="Bates"/>

==Early political career==
Mills was appointed as Maine's first female criminal prosecutor by Governor [[Joseph E. Brennan|Joe Brennan]],<ref name="GlassCeiling" /> and was an assistant attorney general from 1976 to 1980, prosecuting homicides and other major crimes. In 1980, she was elected district attorney for [[Androscoggin County, Maine|Androscoggin]], [[Franklin County, Maine|Franklin]] and [[Oxford County, Maine|Oxford]] counties, a position to which she was reelected three times. She was the first woman district attorney in [[New England]].<ref name="GlassCeiling">{{cite news|title=Janet Mills' mission: Break yet another glass ceiling|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2018/09/16/janet-mills-mission-break-yet-another-glass-ceiling/|newspaper=[[Portland Press Herald]]|date=September 16, 2018|first=Colin|last=Woodard|access-date=November 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181119051649/https://www.pressherald.com/2018/09/16/janet-mills-mission-break-yet-another-glass-ceiling/|archive-date=November 19, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> In [[1994 United States House of Representatives elections#Maine|1994]], Mills was an unsuccessful candidate for the [[United States Congress]] in [[Maine's 2nd congressional district]], losing the Democratic primary to [[John Baldacci]].<ref name="HardTimes">{{cite web | last=Walsh | first=Barbara A. | title=Maine's governor born for hard times, and hard decisions | website=The Maine Monitor | date=9 August 2020 | url=https://themainemonitor.org/maines-governor-born-for-hard-times-and-hard-decisions/ | access-date=24 February 2025}}</ref>

Mills co-founded the [[Maine Women's Lobby]] and was elected to its board of directors in 1998.<ref>{{cite news|title=Mills elected to Maine Women's Lobby board|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=jc4gAAAAIBAJ&pg=1322,3862028&dq=maine-women%27s-lobby&hl=en|newspaper=Sun Journal|date=December 22, 1998}}</ref>

In 2000, Mills served as a field coordinator for [[Bill Bradley]]'s [[Bill Bradley 2000 presidential campaign|2000 presidential campaign]] in Maine.<ref>{{cite web | title= Bill Bradley for President| website=billbradley.com | date=13 October 1999 | url=http://www.billbradley.com/ | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20000229043212/http://www.billbradley.com/ | archive-date=29 February 2000 | url-status=dead | access-date=24 February 2025}}</ref> In 2002, she was elected to the [[Maine House of Representatives]]. There, she served on the [[judiciary]], [[criminal justice]], and [[appropriation (law)|appropriations]] committees. She was reelected in 2004, 2006, and 2008.

=== Attorney general of Maine ===
[[File:Janet Mills Maine AG 2013.jpg|thumb|Mills in 2013]]
Mills was elected to her fourth term when the Joint Convention convened in December 2008 to elect the new attorney general. She became Maine's 55th attorney general on January 6, 2009.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://bangordailynews.com/2009/01/06/news/janet-mills-takes-oath-as-mainersquos-first-female-ag/|title=Janet Mills takes oath as Maine's first female AG|first=Judy|last=Harrison|newspaper=Bangor Daily News|date=January 6, 2009|access-date=September 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170913135421/http://bangordailynews.com/2009/01/06/news/janet-mills-takes-oath-as-mainersquos-first-female-ag/|archive-date=September 13, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref> When Republicans gained control of the Maine legislature in 2010, Mills was not reelected. In January 2011, she was elected vice chair of the [[Maine Democratic Party]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://bangordailynews.com/2011/01/24/politics/portland-lawyer-chosen-as-maine-dems-chairman/|title=Portland lawyer chosen as Maine Dems chairman|newspaper=[[Bangor Daily News]]|date=January 24, 2011|access-date=February 12, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150809100154/http://bangordailynews.com/2011/01/24/politics/portland-lawyer-chosen-as-maine-dems-chairman/|archive-date=August 9, 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> She joined the law firm Preti Flaherty in February 2011 as a lawyer with the firm's Litigation Group in its [[Augusta, Maine|Augusta]] office.<ref>[http://www.preti.com/Former-Attorney-General-Janet-Mills-Joins-Preti-Flaherty "Former Maine Attorney General Janet Mills Joins Preti Flaherty"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150212045812/http://www.preti.com/Former-Attorney-General-Janet-Mills-Joins-Preti-Flaherty |date=February 12, 2015 }}, Preti Flaherty</ref> After Democrats regained control of the legislature in the 2012 elections, Mills was again chosen as attorney general, resigned as vice chair of the Maine Democratic Party,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mainedems.org/press/ben-grant-re-elected-chair-maine-democratic-party|title=Ben Grant Re-Elected as Chair of the Maine Democratic Party|publisher=[[Maine Democratic Party]]|access-date=February 12, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150212050302/http://www.mainedems.org/press/ben-grant-re-elected-chair-maine-democratic-party|archive-date=February 12, 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref> and took the oath of office as attorney general on January 7, 2013.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.kjonline.com/news/Maine-constitutional-officials-to-take-oaths-.html|title=Maine constitutional officials to take oaths|publisher=Central Maine|date=January 7, 2013}}</ref> She was reelected on December 3, 2014, despite the Maine Senate coming under Republican control.<ref>{{cite news|title=Hayes wins election as state treasurer|url=http://www.centralmaine.com/2014/12/03/hayes-wins-election-as-state-treasurer/|newspaper=[[Kennebec Journal]]|date=December 4, 2014|first=Steve|last=Mistler|access-date=September 6, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180809025644/https://www.centralmaine.com/2014/12/03/hayes-wins-election-as-state-treasurer/|archive-date=August 9, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Maine Republican Party|Republican]] governor [[Paul LePage]] opposed Mills for attorney general due to many disputes between them over the legality of some of LePage's policies.<ref>{{cite news|title=Split Legislature creates uncertain re-election path for Democrats in Maine constitutional offices|url=http://bangordailynews.com/2014/11/26/politics/split-legislature-creates-uncertain-re-election-path-for-democrats-in-maine-constitutional-offices/?ref=search|newspaper=[[Bangor Daily News]]|date=December 5, 2014|access-date=November 26, 2014|first=Christopher|last=Cousins|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141210064332/http://bangordailynews.com/2014/11/26/politics/split-legislature-creates-uncertain-re-election-path-for-democrats-in-maine-constitutional-offices/?ref=search|archive-date=December 10, 2014|url-status=live}}</ref> On January 28, 2015, he requested the [[Maine Supreme Judicial Court]]'s opinion as to whether the governor's office needed the attorney general's office's permission to retain outside counsel when the attorney general declines to represent the State in a legal matter. LePage did so after Mills twice declined to represent him in matters she determined had little legal merit, though she approved his requests for outside lawyers.<ref>{{cite news|title=Maine high court to weigh questions about AG Mills' refusal to represent LePage|url=http://bangordailynews.com/2015/01/28/politics/maine-high-court-to-weigh-questions-about-ag-mills-refusal-to-represent-lepage/|newspaper=[[Bangor Daily News]]|date=January 28, 2015|access-date=January 29, 2015|first=Mario|last=Moretto|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150801012616/http://bangordailynews.com/2015/01/28/politics/maine-high-court-to-weigh-questions-about-ag-mills-refusal-to-represent-lepage/|archive-date=August 1, 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> On May 1, 2017, LePage sued Mills, asserting that she had abused her authority by refusing to represent the state in legal matters, or taking a legal view contrary to the LePage administration's.<ref>{{cite news|title=LePage sues attorney general for not representing his legal positions|url=http://bangordailynews.com/2017/05/01/politics/lepage-sues-attorney-general-for-not-representing-his-legal-positions/|newspaper=[[Bangor Daily News]]|date=May 1, 2017|access-date=May 1, 2017|first=Christopher|last=Cousins|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170507004115/http://bangordailynews.com/2017/05/01/politics/lepage-sues-attorney-general-for-not-representing-his-legal-positions/|archive-date=May 7, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Governor of Maine==
=== Elections ===
====2018====
{{main|2018 Maine gubernatorial election}}

On July 10, 2017, Mills announced that she would seek the Democratic nomination for governor of Maine in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.pressherald.com/2017/07/10/maines-attorney-general-janet-mills-says-shell-run-for-governor-in-2018/|title=Maine Attorney General Janet Mills enters 2018 race for governor - Portland Press Herald|last=Thistle|first=Scott|date=July 10, 2017|website=Press Herald|access-date=July 15, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170713084407/http://www.pressherald.com/2017/07/10/maines-attorney-general-janet-mills-says-shell-run-for-governor-in-2018/|archive-date=July 13, 2017|url-status=live}}</ref> One of several candidates in the primary, she won the nomination in June, finishing first after four rounds of ranked-choice voting gave her 54% to her closest competitor's 46%.<ref>{{cite news |last=Shepherd |first=Michael |date=June 20, 2018 |title=Mills wins Maine Democratic gubernatorial nomination after ranked-choice count |url=https://bangordailynews.com/2018/06/20/politics/mills-wins-maine-democratic-gubernatorial-nomination-after-ranked-choice-count/ |work=Bangor Daily News |location=Bangor, ME |access-date=June 21, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191229064738/https://bangordailynews.com/2018/06/20/politics/mills-wins-maine-democratic-gubernatorial-nomination-after-ranked-choice-count/ |archive-date=December 29, 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the general election, Mills faced Republican nominee [[Shawn Moody]], independent [[Maine State Treasurer]] [[Terry Hayes (politician)|Terry Hayes]], and independent businessman Alan Caron. Endorsed by every major newspaper in Maine and the ''[[Boston Globe]]'', buoyed by major ad buys from Democratic political action committees and receiving Caron's endorsement a week before the polls closed, Mills was elected with 50.9% of the vote to Moody's 43.2%. She became Maine's first female governor, the first Maine gubernatorial candidate to be elected with at least 50% of the vote since [[Angus King]] in [[1998 Maine gubernatorial election|1998]], and the first to win at least 50% of the vote for a first term since [[Kenneth M. Curtis]] in [[1966 Maine gubernatorial election|1966]]. She received over 320,000 votes, more than any governor in the state's history.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2018/11/10/gov-elect-janet-mills-announces-transition-team/|title=Gov.-elect Janet Mills announces transition team - Portland Press Herald|date=November 10, 2017|website=Press Herald|access-date=November 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181118104420/https://www.pressherald.com/2018/11/10/gov-elect-janet-mills-announces-transition-team/|archive-date=November 18, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

Mills's campaign was aided in part by a Democratic super PAC that financed Maine-themed ads meant to attract young voters on social media.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2018/09/07/democratic-super-pac-drops-almost-500k-to-back-janet-mills-for-governor/|title=Democratic super PAC spends almost $500,000 to back Janet Mills for Governor - Portland Press Herald|last=Gallagher|first=Noel K.|date=September 7, 2018|website=Press Herald|access-date=November 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181120015202/https://www.pressherald.com/2018/09/07/democratic-super-pac-drops-almost-500k-to-back-janet-mills-for-governor/|archive-date=November 20, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> Both Mills and outside groups outspent Moody by an average of $15 per vote cast, for a total of $10.7 million.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2018/11/18/maines-cd2-race-sets-state-spending-record-with-31m-in-spending/|title=Most per vote, $131, spent on Golden in 2nd District - Portland Press Herald|last=Gallagher|first=Noel K.|date=November 18, 2018|website=Press Herald|access-date=November 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181118124147/https://www.pressherald.com/2018/11/18/maines-cd2-race-sets-state-spending-record-with-31m-in-spending/|archive-date=November 18, 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

====2022====
{{main|2022 Maine gubernatorial election}}

[[File:Janet Mills Inauguration 2023.jpg|alt=Governor Janet Mills in a white pants suit and LL Bean boots with her left hand on a bible and her right hand raised. She is surrounded by other state government employees on a stage.|thumb|Janet Mills being inaugurated for her second term as Governor of Maine]]
Mills ran for reelection in 2022.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Andrews |first=Caitlin |date=September 15, 2021 |title=Most Mainers like Janet Mills' pandemic policies. Paul LePage is still a tough matchup. |work=[[Bangor Daily News]] |url=https://bangordailynews.com/2021/09/15/politics/most-mainers-like-janet-mills-pandemic-policies-paul-lepage-is-still-a-tough-matchup/ |access-date=September 15, 2021 }}</ref> She faced no opposition in the primaries, making her the Democratic nominee. In the general election, Mills defeated the Republican nominee, former governor [[Paul LePage]], securing a second term.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.npr.org/2022/11/09/1134198072/maine-election-results-governor-paul-lepage-janet-mills | title=Maine Gov. Janet Mills wins second term, defeating former GOP Gov. Paul LePage | website=NPR | date=November 9, 2022 | last1=Mistler | first1=Steve }}</ref> She received 376,934 votes, breaking the record for the most votes ever cast for a gubernatorial candidate, set four years earlier.<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 8, 2022 |title=Maine Governor Election Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-maine-governor.html |access-date=July 11, 2024 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Maine Governor election results |url=https://www.politico.com/2022-election/results/maine/statewide-offices/ |website=Politico}}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
One of Mills's first acts as governor was to sign an [[executive order]] to carry out the expansion of Maine's [[Medicaid]] program as called for by a [[2017 Maine Question 2|2017 referendum]], something LePage had refused to do. Medicaid expansion was an issue she had campaigned on.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.centralmaine.com/2019/01/03/mills-signs-executive-order-to-implement-medicaid-expansion/|title=Mills' 'Executive Order 1' makes 70,000 more Mainers eligible for health insurance|last=Lawlor|first=Joe|date=January 3, 2019|website=Kennebec Journal|access-date=January 6, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190104030903/https://www.centralmaine.com/2019/01/03/mills-signs-executive-order-to-implement-medicaid-expansion/|archive-date=January 4, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> Mills also dropped work requirements for Medicaid that LePage requested toward the end of his tenure and that had the [[First presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]]'s approval. She said the work requirements "leave more Maine people uninsured without improving their participation in the workforce".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2019/01/22/mills-rejects-work-requirements-lepage-sought-for-medicaid-beneficiaries/|title=Maine Gov. Mills rejects work requirements LePage sought for Medicaid|first=Joe|last=Lawlor|date=January 22, 2019|website=Press Herald|access-date=January 29, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190129181855/https://www.pressherald.com/2019/01/22/mills-rejects-work-requirements-lepage-sought-for-medicaid-beneficiaries/|archive-date=January 29, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

Mills revived the tradition of Maine governors attending [[Martin Luther King Jr. Day]] commemoration events in [[Portland, Maine|Portland]], doing so in 2019.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.newscentermaine.com/article/news/politics/mills-is-first-governor-to-attend-portland-mlk-event-in-8-years/97-ea5c5143-c682-4c3e-83f1-14ed1c3216aa|title=Mills is first governor to attend Portland MLK event in 8 years|last=York|first=Samantha|date=January 21, 2019|website=newscentermaine.com|access-date=January 22, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123224221/https://www.newscentermaine.com/article/news/politics/mills-is-first-governor-to-attend-portland-mlk-event-in-8-years/97-ea5c5143-c682-4c3e-83f1-14ed1c3216aa|archive-date=January 23, 2019|url-status=live}}</ref>

In September 2019, [[United Nations]] [[Secretary-General of the United Nations|Secretary-General]] [[António Guterres]] asked Mills to speak at the [[United Nations General Assembly|General Assembly]] on [[climate change]]. Mills told world leaders at the UN that she intends to make Maine [[carbon neutral]] by 2045.<ref>{{cite news |last=Woodard |first=Colin |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2019/09/23/at-u-n-gov-mills-vows-maine-will-be-carbon-neutral-by-2045/ |title=In address, Gov. Mills vows Maine will be carbon neutral by 2045, and challenges others |work=[[Portland Press Herald]] |date=September 23, 2019 |access-date=September 25, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190925135837/https://www.pressherald.com/2019/09/23/at-u-n-gov-mills-vows-maine-will-be-carbon-neutral-by-2045/ |archive-date=September 25, 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref> She was the first sitting Maine governor to address the General Assembly.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://bangordailynews.com/2019/09/23/politics/in-un-address-janet-mills-pledges-maine-will-be-carbon-neutral-by-2045/|title=In UN address, Janet Mills pledges Maine will be carbon-neutral by 2045|last=Shepherd|first=Michael|date=September 23, 2019|website=bangordailynews.com|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190924213333/https://bangordailynews.com/2019/09/23/politics/in-un-address-janet-mills-pledges-maine-will-be-carbon-neutral-by-2045/|archive-date=September 24, 2019|access-date=September 24, 2019}}</ref>

On June 11, 2021, Mills announced the end of the [[state of emergency]] started on March 15, 2020, due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Governor Mills Announces State of Civil Emergency To End June 30th {{!}} Office of Governor Janet T. Mills|url=http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/governor-mills-announces-state-civil-emergency-end-june-30th-2021-06-11|access-date=November 12, 2021|website=www.maine.gov|date=June 11, 2021 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=June 30, 2021|title=What the end of Janet Mills' state of emergency means for Maine|url=http://bangordailynews.com/2021/06/30/politics/what-the-end-of-janet-mills-state-of-emergency-means-for-maine/|access-date=November 12, 2021|website=Bangor Daily News|language=en-US}}</ref> The state of emergency ended on June 30, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Governor Mills Welcomes End of State of Civil Emergency {{!}} Office of Governor Janet T. Mills|url=http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/governor-mills-welcomes-end-state-civil-emergency-2021-06-30|access-date=November 12, 2021|website=www.maine.gov|date=June 30, 2021 |language=en}}</ref>

On June 24, 2021, Mills vetoed seven bills, including one that would have closed the [[Long Creek Youth Development Center]], a [[juvenile prison]]. The vetoes received harsh rebuke from progressive Democrats in the legislature.<ref name="bangor020202">{{Cite web |last=Caitlin |first=Andrews |date=June 24, 2021 |title=Janet Mills' veto spree begins, dooming bills to close youth jail and limit pro-corridor spending |url=https://bangordailynews.com/2021/06/24/politics/daily-brief/janet-mills-veto-spree-begins-dooming-bills-to-close-youth-jail-and-limit-pro-corridor-spending/ |access-date=June 26, 2021 |website=Bangor Daily News}}</ref>

On April 20, 2022, Mills signed into law the Maine state supplemental budget, which included free [[community college]] for students of the class of 2020, 2021, 2022 and 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hogan |first=Norah |date=April 22, 2022 |title=Community college will be free for some Maine students |url=https://www.wmtw.com/article/community-college-will-be-free-for-some-maine-students/39790199 |access-date=April 24, 2022 |website=WMTW |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=April 21, 2022 |title=Maine community colleges to offer free tuition for pandemic-affected students |url=https://wgme.com/news/local/maine-community-colleges-to-offer-free-tuition-for-pandemic-affected-students |access-date=April 24, 2022 |website=WGME |language=en}}</ref>

== 2026 U.S. Senate candidacy ==
{{Main|2026 United States Senate election in Maine}}

In December 2022, a month after her reelection as governor, Mills told the ''[[Portland Press Herald]]'' she did not "plan to run for anything else".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Billings |first=Randy |date=2024-11-29 |title=Gov. Mills doesn't rule out challenging Susan Collins for U.S. Senate in 2026 |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2024/11/29/gov-mills-doesnt-rule-out-challenging-susan-collins-for-u-s-senate-in-2026/ |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=The Portland Press Herald |language=en-US}}</ref> In November 2024, the same paper reported that she would not rule out a 2026 campaign for Maine's [[United States Senate]] seat held by five-term incumbent Republican [[Susan Collins]]. Collins is the only Republican representing a state [[Donald Trump]] failed to win in any of his three presidential campaigns.

In August 2025, [[Axios (website)|''Axios'']] reported that Democratic Senate leader [[Chuck Schumer]] was actively recruiting Mills to challenge Collins.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Neukam |first=Stephen |date=2025-08-06 |title=Senate Democrats' top remaining targets for the 2026 midterms |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/08/06/senate-democrats-2026-elections-candidates |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref> Later that month, Mills told reporters she might decide whether to enter the race in November.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Billings |first=Randy |date=2025-08-27 |title=Janet Mills says decision on challenge to Susan Collins may come in November |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2025/08/27/janet-mills-says-decision-on-possible-susan-collins-challenge-may-come-in-november/ |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=The Portland Press Herald |language=en-US}}</ref>

August also saw the campaign launch of [[Sullivan, Maine|Sullivan]] Harbor Master [[Graham Platner]], running in the Democratic primary on a progressive-populist platform. Platner was endorsed by Senator [[Bernie Sanders]] and organized labor.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Shapiro |first=Lisa Wood |date=2025-09-25 |title=Can a Maine Oyster Farmer Defeat a Five-Term Republican Senator? |url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/the-political-scene/can-a-maine-oyster-farmer-defeat-a-five-term-republican-senator |access-date=2025-10-11 |magazine=The New Yorker |language=en-US |issn=0028-792X}}</ref> In October, Sanders publicly discouraged Mills from challenging Platner,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Parks |first=Molly |date=2025-10-09 |title=Sanders publicly discourages Janet Mills run for Maine Senate seat |url=https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/news/campaigns/congressional/3844631/bernie-sanders-publicly-discourages-janet-mills-maine-senate-campaign-2026/ |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=Washington Examiner |language=en-US}}</ref> who had raised over $3.2 million from small donors in the seven weeks since his campaign launch.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Otterbein |first=Holly |date=2025-10-01 |title=Scoop: Bernie-backed oyster farmer raises $3.2M in Maine Senate race |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/10/01/maine-senate-graham-platner-fundraising-sanders |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref> ''Axios'' reported on October 7 that Mills was planning to enter the race by the end of the month<ref>{{cite news |last1=Neukam |first1=Stephen |date=October 7, 2025 |title=Scoop: Mills poised to jump into Maine Senate race |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/10/07/maine-janet-mills-poised-senate-race-announcement |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251007213408/https://www.axios.com/2025/10/07/maine-janet-mills-poised-senate-race-announcement |archive-date=October 7, 2025 |access-date=October 10, 2025 |publisher=[[Axios (website)|Axios]]}}</ref> and on October 10 that Mills would formally enter the race on October 14, citing a leaked campaign document.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Neukam |first1=Stephen |last2=Otterbein |first2=Holly |date=2025-10-09 |title=Scoop: Janet Mills to launch Senate bid Tuesday, per internal document |url=https://www.axios.com/2025/10/09/us-senate-janet-mills-maine-campaign |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=Axios |language=en}}</ref> The same day, an [[ActBlue]] page was launched and a fundraising video was posted to [[Twitter]], but both were deleted.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Pinedo |first1=Peter |last2=Steinhauser |first2=Paul |date=2025-10-10 |title=Democrat prematurely announces Senate campaign launch, but quickly deletes post |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/democrat-prematurely-announces-senate-campaign-launch-quickly-deletes-post |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=Fox News |language=en-US}}</ref>

Mills formally announced her candidacy on October 14.<ref name=":2" /> She said she planned to serve only one term if elected.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pindell |first=James |date=October 14, 2025 |title=Maine Governor Janet Mills launches Senate bid, stressing electability and pledge to serve only one term - The Boston Globe |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/2025/10/14/nation/janet-mills-susan-collins-senate/ |access-date=2025-10-14 |website=BostonGlobe.com |language=en-US}}</ref> If elected, she would have been the oldest freshman senator (surpassing [[Peter Welch]] of [[Vermont]]) and the oldest female senator (surpassing [[Marsha Blackburn]] of [[Tennessee]]) in U.S. history.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Bradner |first1=Eric |last2=Dovere |first2=Edward-Isaac |date=2025-10-14 |title=Maine Gov. Janet Mills' bid for Senate reignites Democrats' age debate |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/10/14/politics/janet-mills-maine-senate-age-debate |access-date=2025-10-15 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref> In a campaign launch video, she highlighted her opposition to Donald Trump with clips of [[Janet Mills#LGBT rights|their confrontation]] in February 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Beaumont |first=Thomas |last2=Whittle |first2=Patrick |date=October 14, 2025 |title=Maine Gov. Mills plans to confront President Trump again with her campaign for the US Senate |url=https://apnews.com/article/maine-mills-senate-trump-collins-e669e25547d5343cee5c3431e14e09b4 |access-date=October 27, 2025 |website=[[AP News]] |language=en}}</ref> Mills was endorsed by Schumer and [[Catherine Cortez Masto]], among other federal lawmakers.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rego |first=Max |date=October 21, 2025 |title=Schumer backs Mills in Maine Senate race, pitting him against Sanders |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/5565965-schumer-endorses-mills-maine/ |access-date=October 27, 2025 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=McCaskill |first=Nolan D. |date=October 14, 2025 |title=Democratic Maine Governor Janet Mills takes on US Senator Susan Collins |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/democratic-maine-governor-janet-mills-takes-us-senator-susan-collins-2025-10-14/ |access-date=October 14, 2025 |website=[[Reuters]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-04-10 |title=Is Chuck Schumer leaving his top candidate recruit twisting in the wind? |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/04/10/democrats-denate-maine-schumer-mills-platner/ |access-date=2026-04-13 |work=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> After she announced her candidacy, Democratic candidates Dan Kleban and Daira Smith-Rodriguez left the race and endorsed Mills.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Davis |first=Emma |date=October 24, 2025 |title=Another Senate candidate drops out to endorse Mills as Platner controversies stack up |url=https://mainemorningstar.com/2025/10/24/another-senate-candidate-drops-out-to-endorse-mills-as-platner-controversies-stack-up/ |access-date=October 27, 2025 |website=[[Maine Morning Star]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

Upon announcing her Senate candidacy, Mills moved to the left on key policy issues, supporting a millionaire's tax (which she had previously opposed) and a moratorium on new [[artificial intelligence]] data center construction.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Steve Mistler |first=Maine Public |date=2026-04-03 |title=Maine Gov. Janet Mills says she'll back 'millionaires tax' in Democrats' budget plan |url=https://www.wbur.org/news/2026/04/03/maine-mills-millionaires-tax-state-budget-senate |access-date=2026-04-08 |website=www.wbur.org |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=WGME |first=Max Williams |date=2026-04-06 |title=Maine lawmakers consider moratorium on large data centers |url=https://wgme.com/news/local/maine-lawmakers-consider-moratorium-on-large-data-centers-ai-artificial-intelligence-technology |access-date=2026-04-08 |website=WGME |language=en}}</ref> According to the ''[[The Wall Street Journal|Wall Street Journal]]''{{'}}s editorial board, Mills's new policy positions are intended to appeal to the Democratic Party base's "[[left-wing populism]]".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Board |first=The Editorial |date=2026-04-07 |title=Opinion {{!}} The Democratic Race to the Left |url=https://www.wsj.com/opinion/maine-data-centers-janet-mills-graham-platner-ac7bbf92 |access-date=2026-04-08 |website=The Wall Street Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> In April 2026, Mills released ads focusing on Platner's history of controversial online comments.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Roarty |first=Alex |date=2026-04-13 |title=Even Some of Janet Mills' Allies Think Time Is Running Out |url=https://www.notus.org/2026-election/janet-mills-graham-platner-maine-senate-time |access-date=2026-04-13 |website=NOTUS |language=en-US}}</ref>

On April 30, 2026, Mills announced that she was suspending her U.S. Senate campaign, saying she no longer had the financial resources to compete against Platner.<ref name="MillsWithdrew" /> Platner was leading in the polls and in fundraising.{{refn|Multiple sources:
<ref name="sp">{{cite web |title=2026 Senate Polling Average – Track All the Latest Polls |url=https://www.racetothewh.com/senate/26polls |website=Race to the WH |access-date=June 9, 2025}}</ref><ref name="dp">{{cite web |title=2026 Maine Senate – Democratic Primary |url=https://www.realclearpolling.com/polls/senate/democratic-primary/2026/maine |website=RealClearPolling |access-date=June 9, 2026 |language=en |archive-date=June 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260604105053/https://www.realclearpolling.com/polls/senate/democratic-primary/2026/maine |url-status=deviated}}</ref><ref name="QuantusMar2026">{{cite web |title=Maine Senate 2026: Collins Faces Uncertain Path to Re-Election|url=https://quantusinsights.org/f/maine-senate-2026-collins-faces-uncertain-path-to-re-election|work=Quantus Insights|date=March 9, 2026|access-date=March 9, 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="PARMar2026">{{cite web |title=68th Omnibus Poll|url=https://drive.google.com/viewerng/viewer?url=https://21701012.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hubfs/21701012/20260304%252068th%2520Omnibus%2520Poll%2520March%25202026.pdf&wmode=opaque|work=Pan Atlantic Research|date=March 4, 2026|access-date=March 4, 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="UNHFeb2026">{{cite web|url=https://scholars.unh.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1929&context=survey_center_polls|title=In Maine Senate Race, Platner Leads Mills in Primary and Collins in General Election Matchup |first1=Sean P.|last1=McKinley|first2=Andrew E.|last2=Smith|first3=Zachary S.|last3=Azem|first4=Tracy|last4=Keirns|website=[[University of New Hampshire]]|date=February 24, 2026|access-date=February 24, 2026}}</ref>}} But Mills never officially withdrew her candidacy, and in late May, she told the ''[[Lewiston Sun Journal]]'' that she "was still on the ballot",<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-06-01 |title=Maine Gov. Janet Mills: 'I am still on the ballot' for Senate |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2026/06/01/maine-gov-janet-mills-im-still-on-the-ballot-for-senate/ |access-date=2026-06-11 |website=The Portland Press Herald |language=en-US}}</ref> though she did not resume active campaigning.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |date=2026-06-05 |title=Janet Mills shows no sign of reigniting her Maine Senate bid amid Graham Platner's troubles |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/janet-mills-shows-no-sign-reigniting-maine-senate-bid-graham-platners-rcna348706 |access-date=2026-06-11 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> Platner won the primary on June 9, with Mills finishing more than 50 percentage points behind him.<ref name=":3" />

== Political positions ==
=== Abortion ===
{{see also|Abortion in Maine}}
Mills has taken steps to expand access to abortion procedures, signing legislation to mandate that both public and private insurance agencies include abortion procedures within the scope of their coverage.<ref>{{cite web |title=Gov. Mills signs bill to make public, private insurers cover abortions |url=https://wgme.com/news/local/gov-mills-signs-bill-to-make-public-private-insurers-cover-abortions |website=WGME |language=en-US |date=June 13, 2019 |access-date=July 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190615213712/https://wgme.com/news/local/gov-mills-signs-bill-to-make-public-private-insurers-cover-abortions |archive-date=June 15, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> After the leak of the 2022 [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]] decision in ''[[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization]]'', Mills reaffirmed her position that "unlike an apparent majority of the Supreme Court, I do not consider the rights of women to be dispensable."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Burns |first=Christopher |date=May 3, 2022 |title=Janet Mills vows to defend abortion rights after draft Supreme Court decision leaks |url=http://bangordailynews.com/2022/05/03/politics/janet-mills-vows-to-defend-abortion-rights-after-draft-supreme-court-decision-leaks/ |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |access-date=May 5, 2022}}</ref>

=== AI data centers ===
In April 2026, a bill to establish an 18-month statewide moratorium on [[AI data center|AI data centers]], the first of its kind in the U.S., was vetoed by Mills.<ref>{{cite web |last1= Soni |first1= Aditya |last2= Dey |first2= Mrinmay |date= April 24, 2026 |title= Maine governor blocks first US state freeze on new data centers |url= https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/maine-governor-rejects-first-us-state-freeze-new-data-centers-2026-04-24/ |website= [[Reuters]] |language= en-US |access-date= July 10, 2026}}</ref> She vetoed the bill because it did not contain a carveout for a planned data center project at the former [[Androscoggin Mill|Androscoggin Paper Mill]] in [[Jay, Maine|Jay]] that she claimed "enjoys strong local support". In June, the $550 million project was put on hold; the data center company, Sentinel, said it did "not intend to move forward with the project".<ref>{{cite web |last1= LaFlamme |first1= Mark |date= June 11, 2026 |title= Plan for $550M data center in Jay on hold, official says |url= https://www.sunjournal.com/2026/06/11/plan-for-data-center-in-jay-suffers-setback-2/ |website= Sun Journal |language= en-US |access-date= July 10, 2026}}</ref> Environmental groups criticized Mills's veto. Maureen Drouin, executive director for Maine Conservation Voters, wrote in a statement that Mills had "sided with large-scale data center developers over safeguards for Maine people and the environment, leaving communities at risk to higher energy prices and more pollution".<ref>{{cite press release |title= Governor Mills vetoes bill to pause large-scale AI data center development in Maine |url= https://www.maineconservation.org/2026/04/24/governor-mills-vetoes-bill-to-pause-large-scale-ai-data-center-development-in-maine/ |website= Maine Conservation Voters |language= en-US |location= Augusta, ME |date= April 24, 2026 |access-date= July 10, 2026}}</ref>

=== Drugs ===
Mills has expressed opposition to the [[drug liberalization|decriminalization]] of drug possession.<ref name= "bangdru2">{{Cite web |last= Andrews |first= Caitlin |date= June 17, 2021 |title= Maine Legislature bucks Janet Mills, police in voting to decriminalize drug possession |url= https://bangordailynews.com/2021/06/17/politics/maine-house-bucks-janet-mills-police-in-vote-to-decriminalize-drug-possession/ |website= Bangor Daily News |language= en-US |access-date= June 20, 2021}}</ref> In 2019, she signed into law the "Good Samaritan Bill", exempting people who report a drug-related medical emergency, including those experiencing a drug-related medical emergency themselves, from criminal liability for possession.<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2019-05-23 |title= Governor Mills Signs Good Samaritan Bill |url= http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/governor-mills-signs-good-samaritan-bill-2019-05-23 |website= Maine Governor's Office |language= en-US |access-date= 2026-04-08}}</ref>

=== Environmental issues ===
{{See also|Climate change in Maine}}
In 2019, Mills signed legislation to ban single-use plastic bags.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Hoey |first1=Dennis |date=June 17, 2019 |title=Gov. Mills signs bill to ban single-use plastic bags |url=https://www.pressherald.com/2019/06/17/bill-signing-makes-maine-the-4th-state-to-ban-single-use-plastic-bags/ |website=Portland Press Herald |language=en-US |access-date=July 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190726181355/https://www.pressherald.com/2019/06/17/bill-signing-makes-maine-the-4th-state-to-ban-single-use-plastic-bags/ |archive-date=July 26, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> She also signed into law a ban on the use of styrofoam containers by various industries within the state. This regulation became effective on January 1, 2021.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Mezzofiore |first1=Gianluca |date=May 1, 2019 |title=Maine becomes the first state to ban Styrofoam |url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/05/01/us/maine-ban-styrofoam-trnd/index.html |website=CNN |language=en-US |access-date=July 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190525234128/https://www.cnn.com/2019/05/01/us/maine-ban-styrofoam-trnd/index.html |archive-date=May 25, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2019, the [[Central Maine Power Company]] (CMP) was seeking permission to construct a 145-mile [[hydropower]] transmission line from a [[Hydro-Québec]] dam system near [[Beatty Township, St. Louis County, Minnesota|Beattie Township]] on the Canadian border to a power grid in [[Lewiston, Maine|Lewiston]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Keefe |first1=Josh |date=January 29, 2019 |title=What you need to know about the CMP transmission line proposed for Maine |url=https://bangordailynews.com/2019/01/29/mainefocus/what-you-need-to-know-about-the-cmp-transmission-line-proposed-for-maine/ |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |access-date=July 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190726185920/https://bangordailynews.com/2019/01/29/mainefocus/what-you-need-to-know-about-the-cmp-transmission-line-proposed-for-maine/ |archive-date=July 26, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The project, known as New England Clean Energy Connect, would be paid for by Massachusetts, and requires a 150-foot-wide, 52-mile-long corridor to be clear cut through the [[Maine North Woods]]. Mills initially expressed skepticism of the proposal during her campaign, and it was criticized by environmental groups, but after several months of negotiations, Mills signed an agreement in support of the project, under which CMP agreed to a package giving Maine $258 million in benefits over 40 years.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Valigra |first1=Lori| date=February 21, 2019  |title=Here are details of the deal that won Janet Mills' support for $1 billion CMP project |url=https://bangordailynews.com/2019/02/21/business/here-are-details-of-the-deal-that-won-janet-mills-support-for-1-billion-cmp-project/ |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |access-date=July 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190726181352/https://bangordailynews.com/2019/02/21/business/here-are-details-of-the-deal-that-won-janet-mills-support-for-1-billion-cmp-project/ |archive-date=July 26, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Terkel |first1=Tux |title=Mills throws her support behind CMP's controversial plan for transmission line |url=https://www.necleanenergyconnect.org/in-the-news/2019/2/21/mills-throws-her-support-behind-cmps-controversial-plan-for-transmission-line |website=New England Clean Energy Connect |language=en-US |date=February 21, 2019 |access-date=July 26, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190726181357/https://www.necleanenergyconnect.org/in-the-news/2019/2/21/mills-throws-her-support-behind-cmps-controversial-plan-for-transmission-line |archive-date=July 26, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Mills has also enacted regulatory standards for the water quality on Indigenous reservations used for sustenance fishing.<ref name= "Sharon 2019">{{Cite web |last=Sharon |first= Susan |date= June 21, 2019 |title=New Measure Establishes Water Quality Standards For Sustenance Fishing In Maine's Tribal Waters |url= https://www.mainepublic.org/post/new-measure-establishes-water-quality-standards-sustenance-fishing-maines-tribal-waters |website= Maine Public |language=en-US |access-date= June 22, 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190622133151/https://www.mainepublic.org/post/new-measure-establishes-water-quality-standards-sustenance-fishing-maines-tribal-waters |archive-date= June 22, 2019 |url-status= live}}</ref>

In 2023, Mills was elected co-chair of the bipartisan Climate Alliance.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-05-05 |title=Governor Mills Elected Co-Chair of U.S. Climate Alliance, Bipartisan Coalition of Governors Leading on Climate Action |url=http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/governor-mills-elected-co-chair-us-climate-alliance-bipartisan-coalition-governors-leading |access-date=2026-04-08 |website=Maine Governor's Office |language=en}}</ref>

=== Filibuster ===
Mills supports retaining the [[Senate filibuster]], the 60-vote threshold for most legislation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kobin |first=Billy |date=October 16, 2025 |title=Janet Mills would support keeping filibuster if elected to the Senate |url=https://www.bangordailynews.com/2025/10/16/politics/elections/janet-mills-support-keeping-senate-filibuster-n6hjn1me0n/ |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |access-date=October 17, 2025}}</ref>

=== Gun control ===
As a state legislator, Mills received A or A+ grades from the [[National Rifle Association]].<ref name="Voght 2018">{{Cite web |last=Voght |first=Kara |date=June 12, 2018 |title=This Democrat received "A+" ratings from the NRA. Today we'll find out if voters care. |url=https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2018/06/janet-mills-adam-cote-nra-maine/ |website=Mother Jones |language=en-US |access-date=October 11, 2025}}</ref> When she ran for governor in 2018, her grade from the same organization was "F".<ref name="Voght 2018"/>

During her 2022 reflection campaign, Mills expressed opposition to banning high-capacity magazines and to strengthening background checks. In the wake of the [[2023 Lewiston shootings]], it was reported that Mills was considering an assault weapons ban.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Blanchard |first=Zach |date=December 19, 2023 |title='We'll do everything we can': Gov. Janet Mills reflects, promises action in wake of Lewiston shootings |url=https://www.newscentermaine.com/article/news/local/governor-janet-mills-lewiston-mass-shooting-interview/97-44cf27c1-57c3-4a55-b43a-9ecd27fa615e |work=News Center Maine}}</ref><ref name= "Tuccille 2024">{{Cite web |last=Tuccille |first=J. D. |date=July 22, 2024 |title=Firearms Policy Coalition takes no prisoners in sharp response to thin-skinned Maine governor |url=https://reason.com/2024/07/22/firearms-policy-coalition-takes-no-prisoners-in-sharp-response-to-thin-skinned-maine-governor/ |website= Reason |language=en-US |access-date=July 24, 2024}}</ref>

In 2025, Mills publicly opposed a ballot referendum to implement a [[red flag law]] in the state, arguing in favor of Maine's existing "yellow flag" law. If there is reason to believe that someone poses a significant danger to themself or others, the yellow flag law, which is unique to Maine, allows the police to ask a judge to order that person to temporarily relinquish their guns after a mental health examination, whereas red flag laws allow a family member to submit a petition to a judge directly, without needing to first go to the police, and without an evaluation. The referendum proposed the red flag law as a supplement to the yellow flag law, not a replacement for it.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Miller |first=Kevin |date=2025-08-28 |title= Maine's 'yellow flag' gun law has been used more than 1,000 times |url=https://www.mainepublic.org/maine/2025-08-28/maines-yellow-flag-gun-law-has-been-used-more-than-1-000-times |access-date=2026-09-24 |website=Maine Public |language=en}}</ref> In an op-ed for the ''[[Portland Press Herald]]'', Mills argued that the proposed law "would create a new, separate and confusing process that will undermine the effectiveness of the law and endanger public safety along with it."<ref>{{Cite web |last=McCauley |first= Lauren |date=September 26, 2025 |title=Gov. Janet Mills comes out against proposed red flag law on November ballot • Maine Morning Star |url=https://mainemorningstar.com/2025/09/26/gov-janet-mills-comes-out-against-proposed-red-flag-law-on-november-ballot/ |website=Maine Morning Star |language=en-US |access-date=April 8, 2026}}</ref> In November, the referendum passed with 62.5% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McCauley |first= Lauren |date=2026-02-21 |title=Maine's new red flag law takes effect |url= https://mainemorningstar.com/2026/02/21/maines-new-red-flag-law-takes-effect/ |access-date= 2026-09-24 |website= Maine Morning Star |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Healthcare===
Upon taking office in 2019, Mills implemented voter-approved [[Medicaid]] expansion, which extended healthcare access to 90,000 residents by 2022.<ref>{{cite news |agency= Associated Press |date= April 4, 2022 |title= Monday marks three years since MaineCare expansion |url= https://wgme.com/news/local/monday-marks-three-years-since-mainecare-expansion |work= WGME-TV}}</ref>

===Education===
Mills's administration fully funded the state's 55% share of public education costs in 2021, and established universal free [[school meal]]s for all public school students.<ref>{{cite web |author=Office of Governor Janet T. Mills |date= April 24, 2026 |title= Expanding Maine's Nation-leading Free School Meals Program |url= https://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/expanding-maines-nation-leading-free-school-meals-program-2026-04-24 |website=Maine.gov}}</ref> Separately, Mills signed legislation making free [[community college]] tuition permanent for future high school graduates, after a pilot program.<ref>{{cite web |author= Maine Community College System |date= April 10, 2026 |title=Governor Mills Signs Supplemental Budget at EMCC, Making Free College Scholarship Permanent |url= https://www.mccs.me.edu/press_release/governor-mills-signs-supplemental-budget-at-emcc-making-free-college-scholarship-permanent/ |website=MCCS Press Releases}}</ref>

===LGBT rights===
Mills supports [[LGBT]] rights.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reed |first=Russ |date=April 1, 2026 |title= Speaker of Maine House Ryan Fecteau blasts Supreme Court ruling in 'conversion therapy' case |url=https://www.wmtw.com/article/maine-speaker-fecteau-supreme-court-conversion-therapy/70908981 |website=WMTW |language= en |access-date=April 8, 2026}}</ref> In May 2019, she signed a bill banning [[conversion therapy]], the [[Pseudoscience|pseudoscientific]] practice aimed at changing one's sexual orientation, from being used on minors.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Ring |first1=Trudy |date=May 29, 2019 |title=Maine Is 17th State to Ban Use of Conversion Therapy on Minors |url= https://www.advocate.com/politics/2019/5/29/maine-17th-state-ban-use-conversion-therapy-minors |website= Advocate |language= en-US |access-date= April 8, 2026}}</ref> A year earlier, the same bill had passed both chambers of the [[Maine Legislature]], but was vetoed by Governor [[Paul LePage]].<ref>{{cite news |last1= Thomsen |first1=Jacqueline |date= July 6, 2018 |title= Maine governor vetoes bill that would ban conversion therapy |url= https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/395909-maine-governor-vetoes-bill-to-ban-conversion-therapy |work= The Hill |language=en-US |access-date= May 29, 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190529232953/https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/395909-maine-governor-vetoes-bill-to-ban-conversion-therapy |archive-date= May 29, 2019 |url-status= live }}</ref>

Mills supports [[Transgender people in sports|transgender athletes' participation in sports]]. On February 21, 2025, during a White House meeting, President [[Donald Trump]] threatened to cut federal funding to Maine if Mills did not comply with [[Executive Order 14201|his executive order]] to prohibit transgender women from participating in women's sports. Mills told Trump "see you in court", and later released a statement saying "The State of Maine will not be intimidated by the President's threats."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Daniels |first=Eugene |date=February 21, 2025 |title=Dem governor tells Trump she will see him in court |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2025/02/21/dem-governor-trump-court-022015 |website=Politico |language=en-US |access-date=February 22, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Benner |first=Katie |date=February 22, 2025 |title=What to Know About Maine Gov. Janet Mills Amid Clash With Trump |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/02/22/us/politics/janet-mills-maine-trump.html |website=The New York Times |language=en-US |access-date=February 24, 2025}}</ref> 

After Mills's exchange with Trump, Maine's Department of Education was unable to access federal funds for a child nutrition program, prompting the state to sue the [[U.S. Department of Agriculture]] over the frozen funds. A federal judge ordered the administration to release the funds. On May 2, 2025, the Trump administration agreed to unfreeze the funds, and Maine dropped the lawsuit.<ref name= "nbc-2may2025">{{cite news |title= Trump administration settles with Maine over funding freeze after dispute over trans athletes |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/nbc-out/out-politics-and-policy/trump-administration-settles-maine-funding-freeze-dispute-trans-athlet-rcna204541 |work= NBC News |language= en-US |date= May 2, 2025 |access-date= May 4, 2025}}</ref> But later that month, the ''[[Portland Press Herald]]'' reported that more than $50 million in federal funding to the [[University of Maine System]] had been frozen or terminated.<ref>{{Cite web |title= Trump administration has frozen $50M in UMaine System funding so far | url= https://www.pressherald.com/2025/05/28/trump-administration-has-frozen-50-million-in-umaine-funding-so-far/ | access-date=2026-09-03 | website=www.pressherald.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title= USDA halts millions in funding for University of Maine System |url= https://www.pressherald.com/2025/03/11/usda-halts-millions-in-funding-for-university-of-maine-system-programs/ |access-date= 2026-08-31 |website= www.pressherald.com}}</ref>

=== Sports betting and online gambling ===
Mills has expressed opposition to [[sports betting]].<ref>{{Cite web |title= Sports betting bill heads to Gov. Mills' desk |url= https://fox23maine.com/news/local/sports-betting-bill-heads-to-gov-mills-desk |website= WGME |language= en-US |date= April 20, 2022 |access-date= April 24, 2022}}</ref> In 2020, Mills vetoed a bill to legalize sports betting in the state; the Maine Senate overrode her veto the next month.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sharp |first=David |date=February 6, 2020 |title=Maine Senate overrides governor's veto on sports betting |url=https://apnews.com/general-news-ccaaa892d7641556ae5722872b3517ed |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref> In April 2026, she signed a bill banning [[Sweepstakes parlor|sweepstakes casinos]] in Maine, and another bill banning the use of credit cards to fund [[sportsbook]] and online casino accounts.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Evans |first= Patrick |date= April 7, 2026 |title= Maine Governor Signs Sweepstakes, Credit Card Bans Into Law |url= https://www.legalsportsreport.com/259771/maine-governor-signs-sweepstakes-credit-card-bans-into-law/ |website= Legal Sports Report |language= en-US |access-date= 2026-04-08}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date= 2026-04-03 |title= Governor signs Malon's bill to protect Maine consumers from gambling addiction {{!}} House Democrats |url= http://www.maine.gov/housedems/news/governor-signs-malons-bill-protect-maine-consumers-gambling-addiction |website= www.maine.gov |language= en-US |access-date= 2026-04-08}}</ref>

=== Tribal relations ===
Mills signed a bill to replace the [[Columbus Day]] state holiday with [[Indigenous People's Day]] and pledged to work to fill seats on a state-tribal commission that had been left empty under her predecessor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mistler |first=Steve |date=April 26, 2019 |title=Mills And State Of Maine Take Steps To Improve Relationships With Maine's Native Tribes |url=https://www.mainepublic.org/post/mills-and-state-maine-take-steps-improve-relationships-maine-s-native-tribes |website=Maine Public |language=en-US |access-date=April 27, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190427002123/https://www.mainepublic.org/post/mills-and-state-maine-take-steps-improve-relationships-maine-s-native-tribes |archive-date=April 27, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> She also signed a bill to establish stricter water quality standards for rivers used by Maine's tribes for sustenance fishing, something long sought by the tribes. It also ended a legal dispute between the tribes and the state, for which Mills as attorney general had defended the state's position.<ref name="Sharon 2019"/>

In 2018, as governor-elect, Mills sent the board of [[Maine School Administrative District 54|Maine School District 54]] a letter urging them to consider Indigenous people's perspectives when deliberating over whether [[Skowhegan High School]] should retire its "Indian" mascot, writing that it has become "a source of pain and anguish" for the Native American community.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Harlow |first=Doug |date=December 6, 2018 |title=Gov.-elect Mills weighs in on Skowhegan schools' Indians nickname |url=https://www.centralmaine.com/2018/12/06/indians-mascot-name-draws-defenders-opponents-at-skowhegan-meeting/ |work=Morning Sentinel |language=en-US |access-date=August 24, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191011114606/https://www.centralmaine.com/2018/12/06/indians-mascot-name-draws-defenders-opponents-at-skowhegan-meeting/ |archive-date=October 11, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> After taking office, she signed a law banning the use of Native American mascots in public schools.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Acquisto |first=Alex |date=May 16, 2019 |title=Mills signs bill to make Maine the first state to ban Native American school mascots |url=https://bangordailynews.com/2019/05/16/politics/mills-signs-bill-to-make-maine-the-first-state-to-ban-native-american-school-mascots/ |website=Bangor Daily News |language=en-US |access-date=August 24, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190616095127/https://bangordailynews.com/2019/05/16/politics/mills-signs-bill-to-make-maine-the-first-state-to-ban-native-american-school-mascots/ |archive-date=June 16, 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Personal life==

In 1985, Mills married real estate developer Stanley Kuklinski and became stepmother to his five daughters. Kuklinski died of a stroke on September 24, 2014.<ref>{{cite news| title= Husband of Maine attorney general dies from effects of stroke| url= http://www.pressherald.com/2014/09/29/husband-of-maine-attorney-general-dies-from-effects-of-stroke/ |newspaper= [[Portland Press Herald]]| date= September 29, 2014| access-date= December 4, 2014| first= Paul| last= Koenig| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20141209112634/http://www.pressherald.com/2014/09/29/husband-of-maine-attorney-general-dies-from-effects-of-stroke/ |archive-date= December 9, 2014 |url-status= live}}</ref> Mills has five grandchildren.<ref name= "aboutthegov" />

Her brother, [[Peter Mills (American politician)|Sumner Peter Mills III]], father, [[S. Peter Mills Jr.]], and grandfather [[Sumner P. Mills]] were all members of the Maine Senate and House of Representatives. Peter Mills III ran for governor of Maine in [[2006 Maine gubernatorial election|2006]] and [[2010 Maine gubernatorial election|2010]], but lost in the primaries both times.<ref name= "Siblings">{{Cite web|first=Tom|last=Bell|date=September 29, 2015|title=Maine's Mills siblings hailed for lives of service|url=https://www.pressherald.com/2015/09/29/mills-siblings-hailed-for-lives-of-service/|access-date=June 24, 2021|website=Press Herald}}</ref> He also served as the [[U.S. Attorney for the District of Maine]] during the Eisenhower, Nixon, and Ford administrations. Her other siblings include Dora Anne Mills, who was a former Director of Maine Public Health, Director of the Maine Center for Disease Control, and Vice President for Clinical Affairs at the University of New England, and Paul Mills, an attorney and writer for Lewiston's [[Sun Journal (Lewiston, Maine)|''Sun Journal'']].<ref name= "Siblings"/> Another brother, David Mills, died in 2024.<ref>{{cite web|title=David Mills, the brother of Maine Gov. Janet Mills, dies at 78|url=https://wgme.com/news/local/david-mills-the-brother-of-maine-gov-janet-mills-dies-at-78|publisher=[[WGME-TV|WGME 13]]|date=January 29, 2024|access-date=March 16, 2026}}</ref>

Mills's primary residence is in [[Farmington, Maine]], where she was born and raised.<ref name="aboutthegov">{{cite web|title=About the Governor|url=https://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/about|website=Office of Governor Janet T. Mills|access-date=July 7, 2025}}</ref> As governor, she resides at the [[Blaine House]], the governor's mansion in [[Augusta, Maine|Augusta]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Governor Mills: Maine is ready to lead|url=https://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/governor-mills-maine-ready-lead-2019-11-27|website=Office of Governor Janet T. Mills|date=November 27, 2019|access-date=July 7, 2025}}</ref>

